      "provenance": {
        "notes": "NAME CLASH: sold as 'hydrangea' with H. paniculata, H. serrata and H. arborescens; pruning and sun differ. This entry is H. macrophylla (mophead and lacecap). Watering: RHS says evenly moist soil, check pots so they do not dry out, move to a shadier spot in summer; no frequency, so interval 3 days is a guess. NC State gives no schedule (occasionally wet soil is fine). Light: RHS light shade, tolerates more sun if the soil stays moist; NC State dappled sun to partial shade, protect from afternoon sun; stored bright_indirect as the closest class. Size: NC State 3 to 6 ft by 3 to 6 ft (upper value 180 cm used); dwarf cultivars 2 to 4 ft; RHS gives no figure; from_knowledge_verify because the upper value of a range was taken and the NC State page was truncated by the fetch (size, growth rate and light are in the verbatim part; USDA zones 6a to 11b and the toxic agent come from a model summary of the cut part, so they are not stored as checked). Growth rate rapid (NC State). Hardiness: RHS hardy through most of the UK, late frost damages buds, no temperature given. Pots: RHS recommends compact cultivars such as 'Red Hot Violet' and 'Altona', ericaceous compost to keep blue flowers on alkaline soil. Toxicity: ASPCA lists toxic to cats and dogs (cyanogenic glycoside; vomiting, depression, diarrhoea; cyanide poisoning rare) but its entry is H. arborescens, so it is only a genus-level reading; NC State's macrophylla page rates severity low with problem tags for cats, dogs and children. Stored 1 (agent's grading). Human skin: RHS says foliage contact may aggravate skin allergies, so adult skin 1; NC State (summary part) says no contact dermatitis, a conflict kept at the cautious grade. Es and it names are machine drafts."
      },
